\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}
We use the DELTA dataset of~\cite{unger2024} unchanged --- 40 repetitions of
the standardized drinking task~\cite{murphy2011} per arm, recorded
simultaneously by optical mocap at $100$\,Hz and by five synchronized webcams
at 1080p and $60$\,Hz --- and report here only the selection applied on top of
it. That selection is stricter about camera quality than an IK-based method
needs to be: with no model prior to absorb it, a miscalibrated view
propagates straight into the 3D point.

\emph{Participants.} Ten participants from the same pool as~\cite{unger2024}.
One was recorded in two sessions under different calibrations, which we treat
separately, giving eleven recording units. Recruitment, inclusion criteria and
ethics approval are as reported in~\cite{unger2024}.

\emph{Cameras.} Cameras were excluded in two passes: first for mis-cut clips,
detected by normalized cross-correlation of each cut clip within its uncut
session recording, and then for miscalibration, decided by leave-one-out
per-joint reprojection. The order is necessary, as a mis-cut clip produces a
smooth, position-dependent reprojection error indistinguishable from a moved
camera --- both land at $25$--$30$\,px. Five cameras were dropped as mis-cut
and seven as miscalibrated, leaving three of the eleven units on all five
cameras and the rest on three or four.

\emph{Trials.} $837$ clips pass an integrity check that the video, its
detections and the resampled mocap span the same window. A trial is then
admitted by a temporal gate --- cross-correlation $\ge 0.7$, best of
$\{$wrist, elbow, shoulder$\} \times \{$speed, displacement$\}$ and the cup,
since raw wrist speed alone rejects sound trials --- and a wrist-validity gate
of $\ge 50\%$ of frames, together admitting $826$ ($99\%$). Of these, $697$
carry a ground-truth row with phase windows and enter the measure comparison
(per-measure $n$ in Table~\ref{tab:measures}), and $687$ the trajectory
comparison. The remainder are absent from the reference processing rather
than failed by ours: the ceiling here is ground-truth coverage.

\subsection{Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}
The pipeline runs in six stages.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \emph{2D body pose.} Stock \texttt{yolo26s-pose}, run per frame on
        every camera.
  \item \emph{Cup localization.} Stock \texttt{yolo26x-seg} locates the cup
        \emph{once}, at the first frame whose detections agree across at least
        three cameras; a transformer visual-object tracker (UETrack-B) then
        follows it through the remaining frames in each camera. It is never
        re-anchored: re-seeding from a shared consensus couples the cameras,
        and can cascade all of them onto the same wrong object, destroying the
        independence that multi-view voting relies on. The cup trajectory is
        used \emph{only} to segment the movement into phases
        (Sec.~\ref{sec:phases}); no movement-quality measure is computed from
        it.
  \item \emph{3D reconstruction.} A multi-view DLT initialises a bundle
        adjustment that minimises geometric reprojection error over all nine
        upper-body joints, weighted by detector confidence and robustified by
        a Huber kernel. The DLT minimises an algebraic residual, which carries
        no statistical interpretation and exists because it is closed-form;
        minimising reprojection error is the maximum-likelihood estimate of
        the joint under Gaussian image noise, and the Huber kernel exchanges
        that for a heavy-tailed model, so a single grossly wrong detection
        cannot dominate the fit~\cite{hartley2004}. Cameras reprojecting
        beyond $30$\,px are excluded and the joint refitted on the rest; where
        too few remain, it is triangulated from all cameras rather than
        dropped, so a degraded view costs accuracy on a frame instead of
        removing the trial. No bone-length prior is used: it holds bone
        lengths constant but slides joints along the unobservable depth ray,
        so reprojection error falls while agreement with OMC worsens.
        \todo{BLOCKED, needs the compute box: log \texttt{n\_fallback} /
        \texttt{n\_guarded} from \texttt{refine\_trial\_ba} over all 826
        trials. The BA solve occasionally diverges and currently reverts that
        joint-frame to the DLT point; the only rate ever measured ($\sim$0.05\%
        of frames, 1 trial in 328) predates the cohort expansion and the
        miscalibrated participants that cause divergence. Decide then whether
        to keep the revert or exclude the trial and report the rate, as
        in~\cite{unger2024}.}
  \item \emph{Temporal smoothing.} SmoothNet, applied to the 3D joint
        trajectories. Its $\pm 16$-frame window is non-causal, which is what
        places this stage after the trial rather than alongside it
        (Sec.~\ref{sec:latency}).
  \item \emph{Phase segmentation.} From the cup trajectory; see
        Sec.~\ref{sec:phases}.
  \item \emph{Measure extraction.} Geometric, from 3D body keypoints only; see
        Sec.~\ref{sec:measures}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase Segmentation}
\label{sec:phases}
The cup is used here and nowhere else. Segmentation reduces to a single
primitive applied to two distance channels: a distance closes, holds flat,
then opens again. On the wrist-to-cup distance that pattern is grasp and
release --- the hand arrives, the distance is flat while the cup is carried,
and opens when it is set down. On the cup-to-mouth distance it is the drinking
phase, as the cup rises, is held, and is lowered. A plateau describes the
relation between two points rather than the motion of either, so the drink
boundaries survive the participant moving the head to the cup instead of the
cup to the head. Reach onset and the final settle come from the hand leaving
and regaining its rest position.

Thresholds are relative: a closing or opening run counts only if it traverses
at least $30\%$ of that channel's own range within the trial, so nothing
depends on the size of the workspace or on an absolute distance to the mouth.
Boundaries are found in one forward pass, each taken as the first qualifying
run after the previous boundary, which makes the phases ordered and
non-overlapping by construction.

The cup is reconstructed under a stricter rule than the body. Frames with
fewer than three agreeing cameras are discarded rather than forced, because a
cup outlier is a different object --- another vessel in the room --- rather
than a displaced joint, and averages to nothing useful. Interior gaps shorter
than $0.75$\,s are filled by a Kalman smoother, never extrapolating beyond the
first or last observation; what remains is filled from the wrist, which
carries the cup, as a constant offset estimated over the frames where the cup
is held. That proxy is applied to the cup-to-mouth channel alone: it makes the
wrist-to-cup distance constant by construction, and grasp and release are read
from exactly that channel.

Two roles for the cup must be kept apart. The deployed pipeline segments from
the cup, but the measure comparison in Sec.~\ref{sec:results} gives both
systems the same optical phase windows, so that those numbers isolate pose
error from phase-classification error. Segmentation is therefore validated
separately, as boundary error against the optical phases
(Sec.~\ref{sec:results}).

\subsection{Movement Quality Measures}
\label{sec:measures}
Measures are computed geometrically from the 3D keypoints; no biomechanical model
is fitted and no inverse kinematics is solved. Throughout, position is smoothed
and then differentiated, never the reverse, since filtering a raw derivative
smears a spike without removing its influence. One threshold is deliberately not
inherited from marker-based practice: movement units are counted against a
$60$\,mm/s amplitude rather than the usual $20$\,mm/s, because the jitter floor of
video sits at $30$--$50$\,mm/s and the lower value manufactures units. The
remaining constants are in the released code.\footnote{\todo{repository URL}}

The eleven measures are those of the drinking-task protocol~\cite{murphy2011};
only the shoulder angles need defining here, since they are computed
geometrically rather than read off a fitted model. An orthonormal body frame is
built from four torso points --- both shoulders and both hips --- giving a down
axis from the shoulder midpoint to the hip midpoint, a frontal-plane normal, and
a lateral axis. Shoulder flexion is the angle of the upper arm from the down axis
within the sagittal plane, abduction the angle within the frontal plane.
\todo{add the Alt Murphy planar-definition reference (Table 2) to refs.bib and
cite it here rather than leaning on~\cite{murphy2011}.}

Three further departures from~\cite{unger2024} are forced rather than chosen.
Their trunk displacement follows a sternum marker, a landmark the COCO keypoint
set does not contain, so ours is the excursion of the shoulder midpoint from its
resting position --- and a three-dimensional excursion rather than an anatomical
forward projection, because that projection needs a forward normal defined by the
hips, which are occluded in a seated participant. The same occlusion is why the
body frame is frozen per trial rather than recomputed per frame: an axis that
jitters dominates the quantity it is meant to reference. Interjoint coordination
changes indirectly, being a correlation involving shoulder flexion. Elbow
extension is unchanged.

Peak velocity is the one departure from the smoothing rule above: it differentiates
the pose without the additional position low-pass and low-passes the resulting
speed instead, because smoothing the position first flattens the peak the measure
exists to report.

Drinking-phase shoulder flexion is not reported, no ground-truth column existing
for it, so eleven measures are reported where~\cite{unger2024} reports twelve.
\todo{This measure is being added. When it lands, update the count in the
abstract, the contributions list, Sec.~\ref{sec:measures},
Sec.~\ref{sec:results-measures} and the Fig.~\ref{fig:correlation} caption --- and
this paragraph goes away entirely, since the percent-timing variants are omitted
by~\cite{unger2024} too and need no explanation from us.}

\subsection{Latency Model}
\label{sec:latency}
We report processing time per trial: the wall-clock interval from the end of a
trial's recording to the emission of its measures, on stated hardware. Per trial
rather than per cohort, so that the figure is comparable across studies of
different size, and because it is the form of the question a clinic asks ---
whether a result arrives within the session that produced it.
\todo{Measurement, not definition: per-stage wall-clock and the total, the
hardware, and whether recording and inference share a GPU. Reported in
Sec.~\ref{sec:results-latency} against the per-trial cost implied
by~\cite{unger2024}, which needed roughly twelve GPU-days for 1160 trials plus
thirty CPU-hours of inverse kinematics on the optical side.}

\subsection{Validation Protocol}
\label{sec:validation}
All measures are computed from our markerless keypoints alone; the optical
system contributes only the values compared against. Where our definitions
depart from~\cite{unger2024} (Sec.~\ref{sec:measures}), the reference is
computed from the optical markers using those same definitions, so that the
comparison isolates pose error rather than reporting a difference of
definition.

Because a marker on the skin and a detected keypoint label different points on
the body, a constant offset between the two is fitted per landmark, in the body
and arm frames, and applied before the angles are compared. This is a
landmark-matching step and not a correction of our estimate: neither system is
positionally authoritative, since the optical markers carry their own placement
error, and comparing unmatched landmarks reports that mismatch as though it were
pose error. The fitted offsets are not themselves reported. Within each
participant the fit is validated on trials held out from it, where agreement
falls within $0.007$ of the in-sample value.
\todo{Generalisation ACROSS participants is untested. Until it is, this is a
within-participant landmark match and must not be described as transferable; a
deployed version would fit against a biomechanical model rather than optical
keypoints.}

The two systems are aligned in time by cross-correlating wrist speed, and the
optical series is shifted onto the video timebase by the resulting lag. For the
per-frame trajectory comparison a static offset is estimated per trial and
removed before agreement is computed, as in~\cite{unger2024}. Unlike the
landmark offsets above, this one is reported: a consistent offset is
correctable and a variable one is not, so its trial-to-trial spread is a result
in itself (Tables~\ref{tab:trajectories} and~\ref{tab:biasiqr}). Per-frame
agreement is then Pearson $r$ and RMSE on the offset-removed series, alongside
the offset and the lag. Per-measure agreement is Spearman $r_s$ over trials and
$r_{av}$ over per-participant, per-arm averages, following~\cite{unger2024}.
Two alignments are therefore reported for the trajectories. Removing a static
offset is the like-for-like comparison with~\cite{unger2024}, but it subtracts
one constant per trial and so leaves the pose-dependent part of the landmark
discrepancy inside the residual. Landmark matching removes that part as well,
and is consequently the better estimate of agreement; it is the alignment used
for the measures throughout.

Both correlations pool across participants and arms, following~\cite{unger2024}.

