\subsection{Phase Segmentation}
\label{sec:results-seg}
The measure comparison above gives both systems the same optical phase windows, so it
says nothing about segmenting from the cup. Table~\ref{tab:boundaries} reports that
separately: the same rule run on the markerless and on the optical cup, paired per
trial. The quantity is the disagreement between the two inputs rather than the error
against the reference phases, because a boundary bias shared by both sides cancels when
markerless measures are compared against optical ones, and so is not what limits the
comparison.

The segmenter declines $37$ of $636$ trials ($5.8\%$). On these no cup frame survives the
three-camera floor, the wrist-to-cup distance is flat, and the grasp collapses onto its
floor at reach onset. Those boundaries are not measurements and are reported as a failure
rate rather than scored: including them leaves the medians unchanged but puts a four-second
tail on release ($p_{95}$ $240$ frames against $15$), which describes the absence of a cup
track rather than the accuracy of a boundary. The failure is detectable without ground
truth, from the cup track alone, which is what makes reporting it possible in deployment.

\input{tables/table4_boundaries}

On the remainder, the boundaries that bound the reaching phase agree closely --- reach
onset to within $33$\,ms and release to within $50$\,ms at the median --- while the two
drink boundaries are the weakest, at $133$\,ms with a fifth of trials beyond $0.25$\,s.
