\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\todo{What the numbers support and what they do not. Once the angles are
landmark-matched, only two measures sit low --- interjoint coordination and
number of movement units --- and both are known to be fragile independently of
markerless capture: \cite{unger2024} reports them as its own two weakest
($0.71$ and $0.77$, against $0.87$--$0.98$ for everything else) and proposes
replacing movement units with log dimensionless jerk, and the study's own
processing treats its movement-unit implementation as unsettled for the same
reason. So the weak pair is a property of the measures, not of the modality.
Add the landmark-offset result here as evidence for the same point from the
other direction: fitting a per-landmark offset lifts the angles to
$0.94$--$0.96$, so the residual was landmark correspondence rather than pose
error, and the optical side is not positionally authoritative either ---
\cite{unger2024} makes the same argument by citation, that $1$--$2$\,cm of
marker registration uncertainty moves a joint angle by $5$--$10$ degrees.
Then: where the remaining error lives, and the clinical implication of
same-session feedback.}

\section{Limitations}
\todo{Disclose the calibration scale error: a displacement-proportional
$\approx 10\%$ inflation of every 3D distance and velocity the pipeline
reports, body keypoints included, which belongs to the camera calibration
rather than to any tracker. Correlational measures are insensitive to it;
absolute ones are not. (The cup's own $\approx 60$\,mm rim-vs-centroid
offset is constant and cancels in the plateau detector, so it needs no
mention unless a cup number is reported.)}

